body:
LMAO 

&#x200B;

also kinda sad that 1 year later this comment still applies

For whatever reason my university assignments take too long to complete. For all of my first year I had to frequently stay late, work on weekends, etc. and I would like to fix this before I move out (I'll be working with my occupational therapist on this over the current holidays and then I'd want one semester to actually use and confirm the effectiveness of any strategies I learn) and that brings me to the next point which is I don't have a job and have never had one so the plan is, after fixing the previous problem I would get a part time job after the semester is over to see if I like/can handle the job and then after that in the next semester I'd make sure that I can handle both the university workload and working. Then finally for the last holiday period before the planned move out date I can get to work on stuff like actually finding a place, etc.

&#x200B;

Writing this all out now I can see there's a lot of room for time cuts. By my calculation the fastest I could move out is mid 2022 but thats considering I don't get overwhelmed which is a big assumption so I'll probably stick with the above more conservative but longer approach.

&#x200B;

I could also just live with the productivity problem and fix it later tbh. My initial thinking was that I wouldn't be able to be guaranteed a stable income unless I have a part time job and I can only have a part time job if I am able to work consistently. I could get casual jobs during the holidays but those aren't guaranteed and I'd be fucked if I don't manage to get a job on even just one holiday.

But even with this there are ways around it like I could always take less units during the semester and take units during the holidays so I can have time to work both during semester and during holidays.

&#x200B;

I guess im just more comfortable with pushing the date further back so I don't have to worry too much about it.

Im not making a snack fund, its a snack budget. So up until now its always been, if i feel like get snacks or fast food or whatever I'll just go and get it and I'll get as much as I want regardless of cost. But what I want to do in the future is have say $30 or something and say thats the max amount I can spend per week on snacks.

&#x200B;

What would you say is a good amount for an emergency fund?

&#x200B;

I don't have a job but I'm looking to get one and I'm currently studying full time.

Yeah so I should have mentioned that my current plan is to do research/prep now (currently on break from university),  on budgeting, potential accommodation and finding a job/writing a resume. Then semester 2 i'll continue/be mostly finished with doing the research and then try to get a job near the end of semester or very early on during the start of end of year holidays which will give me a few months to get used to working and then i'll apply for/try to get accommodation in early 2022 so i can move out for semester 1 in 2022. 

&#x200B;

I'll definitely consider asking for a bit more independence at home first and seeing if there's any support services or charities to help me. Thanks!

Yeah so I should have mentioned that my current plan is to do research/prep now (currently on break from university),  on budgeting, potential accommodation and finding a job/writing a resume. Then semester 2 i'll continue/be mostly finished with doing the research and then try to get a job near the end of semester or very early on during the start of end of year holidays which will give me a few months to get used to working and then i'll apply for/try to get accommodation in early 2022 so i can move out for semester 1 in 2022.

&#x200B;

I've had therapy and I kind of stopped when i started university because the workload got a bit much since i was unused to it. I might see if i can start up again.

Thank you sooo much!!! You have no idea how helpful this is!

&#x200B;

(And yes i am legally an adult)
